Hi all,

People have been getting the "is your posterior discontinuous?" error
message a lot more than I would expect given random initializations.
The reason is that find_reasonable_parameters() was being called
before the parameters were initialized, so it was starting with all
parameters=0.

This bug (which I just pushed a fix for) sometimes results in bad
behavior. I discovered it while playing with the Cauchy distribution
like so:

{{{
parameters {
  real x;
  real y;
}
transformed parameters {
  real xovery;
  xovery <- x / y;
}
model {
  real z;

  x ~ normal(0, 1);
  y ~ normal(0, 1);

  z <- 0;
  z ~ normal(xovery, 1);
}
}}}

Before, the model crashed, because it was trying to find a reasonable
epsilon at the setting x=0,y=0 (which yields a nan). Now it works
fine, because that setting has 0 posterior mass.

BTW, the posterior for x and y in this model (plot attached) looks
like the top of an opened pre-pull-tab beer can.

Matt

