I rewrote VectorView as a single template class VV, but haven't
pushed it or tested it in context yet (just with standalone
tests).  I think it'll work for what we need it for, but would
love to get some feedback:

   template <typename T,
             bool is_array = stan::is_vector_like<T>::value>
   class VV {
   public:
     typedef typename scalar_type<T>::type scalar_t;

     VV(const scalar_t& c) : x_(&c) { }

     VV(const std::vector<scalar_t>& v) : x_(&v[0]) { }

     template <int R, int C>
     VV(const Eigen::Matrix<scalar_t,R,C>& m) : x_(&m(0)) { }

     scalar_t operator[](int i) {
       if (is_array) return x_[i];
       else return *x_;
     }
   private:
     const scalar_t* x_;
   };

I'm saving a direct pointer to the array in the member variable x_.
This could be dangerous, because it doesn't keep up with changes to
the vectorized object.  The original implementation stored a pointer
to the wrapper.

I'm also returning a simple scalar_t.  This could be replaced
with const scalar_t&.

Do we ever need to assign through vector views?  The vector views
in normal.hpp are all const.  If we need to assign, we'll
need to take non-const constructor args and return non-const
scalar_t&.

I needed to add the following specialization for the
scalar extractor metafunction:

   template <typename T>
   struct scalar_type<Eigen::Matrix<T,Eigen::Dynamic,Eigen::Dynamic> > {
     typedef typename scalar_type<T>::type type;
   };

And then I wrote a new metafunction is_vector_like instead of
making is_vector return true for a full matrix (dynamic rows,
dynamic columns).

   template <typename T>
   struct is_vector_like {
     // handles scalar, eigen vec, eigen row vec, std vec through is_vector
     enum { value = stan::is_vector<T>::value };
   };

   template <typename T>
   struct is_vector_like<Eigen::Matrix<T,Eigen::Dynamic,Eigen::Dynamic> > {
     enum { value = true };
   };

